<div class="language-json-doc highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nl">"behavior"</span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">[</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s2">"auto-advance"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s2">"individuals"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">]</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w">
</span></code></pre></div></div>
      <h5 id="timemode">
        
        
          timeMode <a href="#timemode" class="anchorClass"></a>
        
        
      </h5>
    

<p>A mode associated with an Annotation that is to be applied to the rendering of any time-based media, or otherwise could be considered to have a duration, used as a body resource of that Annotation. Note that the association of <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">timeMode</code> with the Annotation means that different resources in the body cannot have different values. This specification defines the values specified in the table below. Others may be defined externally as an <a href="/api/presentation/3.0/#46-linked-data-context-and-extensions" title="Linked Data Context and Extensions">extension</a>.</p>

<p>The value <em class="rfc">must</em> be a string.</p>

<ul>
  <li>An Annotation <em class="rfc">may</em> have the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">timeMode</code> property.<br />
Clients <em class="rfc">should</em> process <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">timeMode</code> on an Annotation.</li>
</ul>

<table class="api-table" id="table-timemode">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Description</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">trim</code></td>
      <td>(default, if not supplied) If the content resource has a longer duration than the duration of portion of the Canvas it is associated with, then at the end of the Canvas’s duration, the playback of the content resource <em class="rfc">must</em> also end. If the content resource has a shorter duration than the duration of the portion of the Canvas it is associated with, then, for video resources, the last frame <em class="rfc">should</em> persist on-screen until the end of the Canvas portion’s duration. For example, a video of 120 seconds annotated to a Canvas with a duration of 100 seconds wo